 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ to scan data for min/max, where $N$ is the number of input points. Constant spacing is $O(1)$ for placement after min/max calculation. Variable spacing is significantly slower, requiring an order of magnitude more CPU time due to maintaining linked lists of unique coordinate values [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_x + N_y + N_z)$ to store plane positions. Variable spacing may require additional memory for bin structures and linked lists [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Constant spacing is fast and simple to implement. It guarantees a regular grid structure suitable for volume visualization [S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Inefficient for sparse data, as it samples empty space uniformly. Variable spacing can lose information in less dense areas if the binning algorithm over-emphasizes dense regions [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Use Constant Plane Spacing for general-purpose volume visualization where performance is key and data is reasonably uniform. Use Variable Plane Spacing only when data density varies drastically and capturing fine details in dense regions is the primary concern [S2, unofficial].
